> Can I estimate a benefit-cost ratio based on an economic internal rate of return?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can estimate a benefit-cost ratio (BCR) from an economic internal rate of return (IRR), but it's not a straightforward conversion. You'll need additional information about the project's cash flows and discount rate. Here's a general approach:
> 
> 1. **Understand the Metrics**:
>    - **IRR** is the discount rate that makes the net present value (NPV) of the project zero. It considers the time value of money and provides a way to evaluate the profitability of investments.
>    - **BCR** is the ratio of the present value of benefits to the present value of costs. A BCR greater than 1 indicates a good investment.
> 
> 2. **Data Required**:
>    - Detailed cash flow information, including both costs and benefits over time.
>    - A discount rate other than the IRR to value these cash flows.
> 
> 3. **Compute Present Values**:
>    - Calculate the present value of both the benefits and costs using a chosen discount rate. If not given, a commonly used rate is the benchmark or required rate of return for similar investments.
> 
> 4. **Calculate BCR**:
>    - Use the formula: 
> 
>      \[
>      \text{BCR} = \frac{\text{Present Value of Benefits}}{\text{Present Value of Costs}}
>      \]
> 
> Since IRR gives you a sense of the project's return in terms of a discount rate, you'll need to choose a discount rate (like a societal discount rate) to discount the actual cash flows and find their present values. By comparing these present values, you can compute the BCR.
> 
> If you don't have access to the detailed cash flows, reverse engineering a BCR from IRR would involve simplifying assumptions which could lead to inaccuracies. Generally, having the full financial data will lead to more reliable estimations.
