> 
> ### Steps to Calculate BCR
> 
> 1. **Assumptions**:
>    - Let \( C \) be the total costs incurred in year 0.
>    - Let \( B \) be the constant annual benefits starting from year 1.
>    - Let \( DR \) be the discount rate.
> 
> 2. **Present Value (PV) of Benefits**:
>    The present value of benefits, which are received for an indefinite period, can be calculated using the formula for the present value of a perpetuity:
> 
>    \[
>    PV(\text{Benefits}) = \frac{B}{DR}
>    \]
> 
> 3. **Present Value of Costs**:
>    Since all costs are incurred in year 0, the present value of costs is simply:
> 
>    \[
>    PV(\text{Costs}) = C
>    \]
> 
> 4. **BCR Calculation**:
>    Now, you can calculate the benefit-cost ratio using the formula:
> 
>    \[
>    BCR = \frac{PV(\text{Benefits})}{PV(\text{Costs})} = \frac{\frac{B}{DR}}{C}
>    \]
> 
>    Simplifying this gives:
> 
>    \[
>    BCR = \frac{B}{C \times DR}
>    \]
> 
> ### Conclusion
> 
